Proctalgia Fugax
Idiopathic & Benign Entities
Sudden severe sharp rectal pain, Ultra-brief episodic duration (seconds to minutes), Frequent nocturnal awakenings, Normal inter-attack digital rectal examination

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IBE-042
Reason for Visit: Acute, agonizing rectal pain causing severe nocturnal awakening and fear of an acute surgical bowel emergency.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient who was abruptly awakened by severe, sharp, deep rectal pain accompanied by profound autonomic distress. Urgent after-hours assessment was mandatory to physically perform a digital rectal examination, definitively rule out an acute perianal abscess, ischemic bowel, or thrombosed hemorrhoid, and safely manage the acute crisis prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I woke up in blinding agony, like a knife in my rectum. It lasted ten minutes and now it's gone, but I was pouring sweat."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents following a sudden, unpredictable onset of severe, excruciating, and sharp localized pain in the deep rectum. The attack intensely awakened the patient from a deep sleep strictly at night. The attack had an ultra-brief episodic duration, resolving completely within 15 minutes. The patient reports an absolute lack of any preceding warning signs or specific triggers. Currently, there is complete, spontaneous, and absolute resolution of the severe pain, but the patient remains highly anxious and refuses to sleep. Patient denies actual localized rectal bleeding, palpable masses, or severe continuous tenesmus.
Functional Status: Currently pacing, exhausted, terrified of experiencing another attack in bed.
Social/Safety Concerns: Profound health anxiety regarding a missed surgical diagnosis; significant sleep disruption.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/82 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Alert, residual pallor, visibly anxious but currently entirely pain-free.
Physical Exam: Abdomen is completely soft, non-distended, and non-tender globally. External perianal inspection reveals normal, uninflamed tissue with no fissures, fistulas, or thrombosed hemorrhoids. Digital rectal examination reveals normal sphincter tone, an empty rectal vault, and a complete absence of localized tenderness, fluctuant abscesses, or masses. Prostate is normal. Documented history of a completely normal anoscopy and flexible sigmoidoscopy effectively ruling out active inflammatory bowel disease or underlying structural neoplastic lesions.
Environmental Observations: Bed covers thrown back in panic; patient is sweating heavily.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Proctalgia Fugax - Status: Resolved acute spastic episode
Differentials:

Levator Ani Syndrome - Ruled out clinically as the pain was sharp, excruciating, and ultra-brief (15 mins), rather than a chronic, continuous, dull aching heaviness.

Perianal/Ischiorectal Abscess - Ruled out by the strictly normal temperature, completely normal external perianal inspection, and absolute lack of localized tenderness on digital rectal examination.

Acute Anal Fissure - Ruled out by the absence of sharp, tearing pain directly associated with a bowel movement, absence of bleeding, and normal, painless digital examination.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient required an urgent after-hours assessment due to a sudden, highly traumatic, and agonizing pain episode that simulated an acute surgical catastrophe (such as a ruptured abscess or ischemic event). Although the pain had resolved by the time of the physical assessment, the patient's severe secondary anxiety and autonomic distress mandated an immediate, hands-on clinical evaluation, strictly including a digital rectal examination. By confirming entirely normal inter-attack anorectal anatomy and ruling out a severe deep space infection, the physician safely diagnosed a benign, transient functional spasm. This definitive clinical exclusion prevented an inappropriate, high-stress emergency department presentation in the middle of the night, strictly satisfying the criteria for MBS Item 597.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Provided immediate, firm clinical reassurance regarding the completely normal physical examination and the benign, self-limiting nature of the condition.
Positive Behavioral Change: During a future attack, immediately draw a very hot bath and sit in it, or firmly press a tennis ball against the perineum while sitting. The intense ambient heat or direct mechanical pressure physically forces the spasming sphincter to reflexively relax, directly rewarding the patient with faster pain resolution and providing a concrete action plan to combat the severe feeling of helplessness during an attack.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Heating pad.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 1 month if frequency of attacks increases.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if developing a high fever with chills, sudden localized swelling or redness around the anus that does not go away, passage of large amounts of blood, or absolute inability to pass urine.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis assumes the pain has completely resolved; any new, continuous, or worsening pain requires immediate surgical re-evaluation.
Clinical Course: Proctalgia fugax is a sudden, violent cramp of the anal sphincter or pelvic floor muscles. It is excruciatingly painful and terrifying, but it is structurally completely harmless and always resolves entirely on its own within minutes.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if you develop a fever, notice a hot, swollen, painful lump near your anus that persists, or if you start bleeding heavily from the bowel.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The extreme pain felt was a severe muscle spasm, identical to a 'charley horse' cramp in the calf, but occurring in the muscles of the pelvis. Because the cramp only lasts minutes, taking a painkiller is completely useless because the pain will be gone before the pill even dissolves.
Actionable Steps: Keep a heating pad next to the bed. The moment an attack causes awakening, immediately sit on the heat pad. Supplying direct thermal energy to the spastic muscle increases blood flow and rapidly breaks the physical cramp, directly shortening the attack and restoring control over the symptoms.

